WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.860
Hi, I'm Briana with freeCodeCamp

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.840
and today we're going to be talking about the

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.460
Binary Numeral System,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.860
also known as a bunch of 1's and 0's

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:08.980
over and over again.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.940
We're going to start by labeling, starting backwards,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.369
how many numbers there are.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:19.760
This will be zero, one, two, three, four, five, six.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.179
Pretty straightforward so far.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.200
The next step that we're going to do is:

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.109
Take two to the power of whatever place it is.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:30.180
So here, it'll be two to the power of zero,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.360
two to the power of one,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.460
two to the power of two,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:33.560
two to the power of three,

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.600
you get the picture.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.180
Alright, now that we've got the two to the given power

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.920
figured out all we got to do is

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.640
fill in some lengths and we'll have our solution.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.060
Two to the power of zero is one.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.480
Two to the power of one is two.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.060
Two to the power of two is four.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.460
We've got 8, 16, 32, 64.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.820
And that just keeps going on and on.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.600
Next what we're going to do is see which ones of

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.879
these numbers do we keep.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.036
So the next step: figure out which numbers do we keep.

00:01:03.036 --> 00:01:04.919
Do we just add all of these together?

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.960
And if these were all ones, then yes we would.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.760
But some of them are zeros.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.620
The zero is the same thing as an off switch.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.680
1 being on, 0 being off.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.340
So we can go ahead and cross all of these out right here

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.540
because they're they're not going to really matter.

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:22.587
We want to keep this one, this one here, and here

00:01:22.587 --> 00:01:25.098
because they have the corresponding 1's

00:01:25.098 --> 00:01:26.039
which means: Yes! It's on!

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.040
We want to keep that number.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.120
Basically from here on out. We're just going to add these

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.280
four numbers together. [Adding] Giving us, we've got 72, 74, 75.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:40.160
So this number in binary means 75.

